# CORE #11/#12 REGRESSION — `for p in d:` over a `Deque[S]` where `S`
# has ≥2 fields silently TRUNCATES to 8 bytes per iteration.
#
# AXIS PIN: Deque[S] multi-field struct payload · `for` iteration ·
# elem-size derivation via `elem_size_from_monomorphized`
# (src/lir/lower/types.rs). Same `Deque__` arm miss as the push /
# index-assign faces.
#
# RED-VERIFIED PRE-FIX (Round XXVI Track D scout 2026-08-02): the
# iterator reads 8 bytes per slot, so `p.x` returns the correct low
# int64 of the FIRST field but `p.y` / `p.z` return bleed-through
# from the next slot (or zero on the last iteration). BOTH backends.
# `deque_for_iter_struct_payload.gg` covers the single-int-field
# sibling — accidentally correct via the 8-byte fallback (Core #15e
# Q6); this fixture is the multi-field axis cell.
#
# INTENDED post-fix: `100 / 200 / 300` then `400 / 500 / 600`.
from std.collections import Deque

struct Point:
    int x
    int y
    int z

void main():
    Deque[Point] d = Deque[Point]()
    d.push(Point(100, 200, 300))
    d.push(Point(400, 500, 600))
    for p in d:
        print(p.x)
        print(p.y)
        print(p.z)
